From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 11:08:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: let the core suspend after a reset

rocket_reset() drops the in-flight job's runtime PM reference with
pm_runtime_put_noidle(), a bare decrement that requests nothing.  The
core is left at usage_count 0 but still runtime-active with no idle
request pending, so it does not suspend until something else asks.  On a
core that takes no further submits, nothing ever does: the clocks
rocket_device_runtime_resume() prepared stay enabled and the domain stays
up for as long as the driver is bound.

It also matches the put in the completion path a few lines away, so the
reset path no longer leaves the device in a state the rest of the driver
never produces.  The other put in this file, on rocket_job_run()'s failed
pm_runtime_get_sync(), stays a bare put_noidle(): it unwinds a reference
to a device that never resumed, so there is nothing to request idle on.

What this does not do on RK3588 is fix an observed failure.  The reason
upstream carries it is a platform whose power domain does work when it
comes up -- on RK3576 a bus interface reset -- where a core that never
suspends never gets that work and the job after a timeout comes back as
the output zero point with rk_iommu reporting MMU_DTE_ADDR not
functioning, 0 of 128 channels against 128 of 128 with the single line
changed.  Igor Paunovic ran the differential here: 45 induced resets
across three cores, with and without, and the domain dropped every time
with no MMU message on either kernel.  So on this part the change is that
the driver stops producing a state it produces nowhere else, and stops
holding a core resumed after a job it has already given up on.

One consequence to know.  pm_runtime_put_autosuspend() is asynchronous
against the autosuspend delay, so on a workload whose gap to the next
submit is shorter than that delay the core does not actually suspend
between the two, and the reset is followed by a resume that never
happened.  Nothing here depends on it having happened; it is the
platforms that do work on power-on that care.

Tested-by: Igor Paunovic <royalnet026@gmail.com>  # RK3588, three cores, induced reset, JOB_TIMEOUT_MS=2
Fixes: 0810d5ad88a1 ("accel/rocket: Add job submission IOCTL")
Signed-off-by: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
[carried for the RK3588 series; the comment this rewrites is the one 091
 leaves behind, and 091 is also what guarantees no threaded handler is
 still running when the core it just made suspendable goes down]
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 6 +++---
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index f61215a19..f970fe893 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -540,12 +540,12 @@ rocket_reset(struct rocket_core *core, struct drm_sched_job *bad)
 
 	/*
 	 * No handler is running now, but we might still have stuck jobs. Let's
-	 * make sure the PM counters stay balanced by manually calling
-	 * pm_runtime_put_noidle().
+	 * make sure the PM counters stay balanced by putting the reference the
+	 * job took, and request idle while doing it so the core can suspend.
 	 */
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
 		if (core->in_flight_job)
-			pm_runtime_put_noidle(core->dev);
+			pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(core->dev);
 
 		core->in_flight_job = NULL;
 	}
-- 
